@model DashboardModule.Areas.Dashboard.Controllers.DashboardView
@{
    Layout = null;
}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <title>Dashboard</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Dashboard</h1>
    <p>Served by a module the host has never heard of.</p>
    <p>@Model.TileCount tiles.</p>
    @*
        Both of these reach the internal view component: the name is looked up at runtime, and the
        generic overload resolves the type here, in a generated view class that is itself internal
        and in this same assembly.

        What does not work is <vc:tiles />. That one is bound by the Razor compiler, which requires
        a public type — and when it does not find one it says nothing at all: the element is copied
        into the page as literal HTML, with no error and no warning.
    *@
    <p>@await Component.InvokeAsync("Tiles")</p>
    <p>@(await Component.InvokeAsync<DashboardModule.Areas.Dashboard.Components.TilesViewComponent>())</p>
</body>
</html>
